<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transvaal Province</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> Province was one of the four provinces of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and later the Republic, formed in 1910 from the Transvaal Colony, the former South African Republic. It contained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, the administrative capital, and the gold-mining city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>. In 1994 it was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpumalanga">Mpumalanga</a>, the Northern Province, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, and part of North West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transvaal-province.jpg" alt="Transvaal Province" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Htonl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_of_Transvaal_Province_as_of_1_March_1994.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> prefix trans, meaning across, and referred to its position north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal River</a>. The province lay between the Vaal in the south and the Limpopo River in the north. To its south were the Orange Free State and Natal, to its west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a> and the Bechuanaland Protectorate, to its north Rhodesia, and to its east Portuguese East Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>. Except on the southwest, the borders were mostly defined by natural features. The Waterberg Massif, an ancient geological feature, lies within it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> was its capital and also the executive capital of the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since at least the 8th century by the Venda and Sesotho people. From 1817 several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> groups, such as the Matabele, who had been expelled from Natal by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a>, invaded the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>. At the end of the 1830s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> left on the Great Trek into the interior, where the South African Republic was founded. It was an independent country ruled by the Dutch-speaking Boers from 1852 to 1902, and the borders of the later province coincided with it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> emerged as major cities in this period. In 1902 the republic was conquered by the United Kingdom and became the Transvaal Colony.</p><p>In 1910 four British colonies united to form the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the Transvaal Colony became the Transvaal Province. In 1961 the union left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a> and became the Republic of South Africa. The conurbation centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, called the PWV, became the country's economic centre. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustans</a> lay wholly within the province, among them Venda, KwaNdebele, Gazankulu, KaNgwane and Lebowa, and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bophuthatswana">Bophuthatswana</a> lay there too. The province was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1994, after the fall of apartheid, a new constitution abolished the former provinces and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> ceased to exist. Its south-central portion, including the PWV, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a>, its northern portion became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> and its southeastern portion became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpumalanga">Mpumalanga</a>. Most of North West came from its southwestern portion, and a small segment joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>. The old regional names Northern, Eastern, Western and Southern Transvaal survive in the names of sporting teams: the Blue Bulls were formerly Northern Transvaal, and the Golden Lions, formed in 1889, were formerly Transvaal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transvaal (province)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Botha and Smuts led the negotiations for union, and in 1910 the Transvaal joined the Union of South Africa as the <b>Transvaal Province</b>, with Pretoria as the administrative capital of the new state.</blockquote><a href="/state/transvaal-colony">Transvaal Colony</a></li></ul></section>
